# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# Two answers about background jobs that only zsh gives — #2318's zsh/ half.
#
# The first is what `$!` holds before anything has been backgrounded. bash,
# ksh93 and dash leave the parameter unset, so `${!-unset}` yields the default
# and a script can tell "no job yet" from "a job whose id is zero". zsh has it
# set to 0 from the start, and a script that writes `if [ -n "$!" ]` to mean
# "have I started one" gets yes on a shell that has started nothing.
#
# The second is the status `jobs` gives a job specification when the table is
# empty. zsh answers 127 — the status of a command that is not there — where
# bash and ksh93 answer 1 and dash answers 2. It is the same 127 zsh spends on
# a `wait` that cannot resolve a spec, so the shell treats "no such job" as
# "no such command" throughout, and a script testing `jobs %1` against 1 to
# mean "nothing running" reads a shell error instead. Every spelling of a spec
# is asked, because the status belongs to the lookup and not to the spelling.
#
# Nothing here prints a pid or a job number. `$!` is compared and used as an
# operand, and written out only where it is 0 or empty and can therefore be
# neither — which is the rule the core file states and the reason most of job
# control is unaskable in a suite with no expected output.
#
# ---- what used to be here, and what it was ----
#
# This file's second answer was `wait` with more than one operand: POSIX makes
# the status the *last* operand's, bash, ksh93 and dash all report that, and
# zsh was written down as reporting the first. That is not a fact about zsh.
# It is the reap window #2963 and #2974 were both caught by, read as a dialect
# answer (#2993). Measured 2026-09-15 with two jobs exiting 3 and 4 in that
# order:
#
#   asked immediately, zsh answers 3 — and it answers 3 with the operands
#     *reversed* as well, so it is not reporting the first operand, it is
#     reporting the older job
#   asked after a builtin-only busy loop, so both children are certainly
#     reaped, zsh answers 4 in start order and 3 reversed — the last operand,
#     exactly like the other three
#
# Which of the two a run gets is wall-clock. Holding the jobs open on fifos
# and releasing them first — the pattern core/jobs.tests uses — does not fix
# it either: 54 runs in 120 split under load, the majority landing on the
# reaped answer. So the question is not askable here in any spelling, and the
# only thing that was ever zsh-only about it was which side of a scheduling
# decision the measurement fell on.

# `$!` before any background command, asked the four ways a script asks it.
# Printed through the default and the alternative, so what is compared is
# "set" against "unset"; printed bare too, because the only two values it can
# have here are `0` and nothing and neither is a pid.
printf 'before any job [%s]\n' "${!-unset}"
printf 'before any job, the alternative [%s]\n' "${!+set}"
printf 'before any job, written out [%s]\n' "$!"
if [ -n "$!" ]; then printf 'non-empty before any job -> yes\n'; else printf 'non-empty before any job -> no\n'; fi

# A job specification against an empty table. Asked in every spelling — the
# current job, its two signs, and two numbers, one of which was never issued —
# because what is being measured is the status of a lookup that found nothing
# and not the grammar of the word.
jobs %% 2> /dev/null; printf 'jobs %%%% with an empty table -> %s\n' "$?"
jobs %+ 2> /dev/null; printf 'jobs %%+ with an empty table -> %s\n' "$?"
jobs %- 2> /dev/null; printf 'jobs %%- with an empty table -> %s\n' "$?"
jobs %1 2> /dev/null; printf 'jobs %%1 with an empty table -> %s\n' "$?"
jobs %2 2> /dev/null; printf 'jobs %%2 with an empty table -> %s\n' "$?"

# The control, and it is what makes the five above a statement about the spec
# rather than about the builtin: `jobs` with nothing after it succeeds on an
# empty table in every column.
jobs > /dev/null 2>&1; printf 'jobs with no spec at all -> %s\n' "$?"

# The other control, and it is the one that says what the 127 belongs to. Give
# `fg` and `bg` the same empty table and the same spec and zsh answers 1, the
# same 1 bash and ksh93 give — so the status is `jobs`'s own answer to a
# lookup that found nothing, not this shell's answer to every missing job
# spec. Both are run with their output caught, because refusing is the case
# and the four sentences they refuse with are not.
fg %1 > /dev/null 2>&1; printf 'fg %%1 with an empty table -> %s\n' "$?"
bg %1 > /dev/null 2>&1; printf 'bg %%1 with an empty table -> %s\n' "$?"

# After one job, `$!` is a number — asked as a comparison, since a pid does
# not reproduce.
( exit 0 ) &
printf 'after one job, positive [%s]\n' "$(( $! > 0 ))"
wait

# One operand is unambiguous everywhere, and it is here as the control for
# what was removed above: `wait` reports the status of the job it was given,
# and it is only with *several* operands that the question stops being a fact.
( exit 5 ) & g=$!
wait "$g"
printf 'one operand -> %s\n' "$?"
